// ============================================================
// Self-balancing robot: R=1.4 / R=1.6 selectable 4-state LQR
//
// Base:
//   User's corrected 1-wheel + 6-stage input-delay observer code.
//
// Main policy of this version:
//   1) Preserve the experimentally best 4-state balance architecture.
//   2) Keep left/right synchronization kWheelSync = 1.20.
//   3) Remove the unsuccessful delta-u controller.
//   4) Compare only R=1.4 and R=1.6 by changing one enum line.
//   5) Keep the optional slow position-hold outer loop disabled by default.
//   6) Keep Serial logging at 20 Hz.
//
// IMPORTANT:
//   angleSpeedAverageA/B behavior is intentionally preserved from the
//   currently stable code. Do not "fix" this into conventional wheel-speed
//   feedback unless the lower loop is retuned from scratch.
//
// 2026-08-16 UPDATE:
//   All model-dependent constants below were regenerated by
//   design_best_R14_controller.py in this directory, for the plant:
//
//       T_pendulum = 0.75 s   (unchanged)
//       h_body     = 0.060 m  (was 0.080 m; does not affect the model)
//       dead time  = 0.110 s  (was 0.100 s, 6 stages)  <-- the real change
//
//   Only the dead time actually changed the model.  h_body and m_body
//   cancel out of both A_c coefficients, and a 0.45 s pendulum period
//   measured with a string turned out to be a two-DOF artifact (a rigid
//   body with h = 0.060 m cannot swing faster than 0.492 s), so the
//   nail-through-the-axle value of 0.75 s is kept.
//
//   Regenerated: A_d / B_d in the observer prediction, the observer gain L
//   in the correction step, and the LQR gains.  The observer gain is the
//   steady-state Kalman gain for Q_obs = I(10), R_obs = I(4), i.e. the same
//   recipe as the previous configuration.
//
//   The controller structure is unchanged.
//
// 2026-08-16 UPDATE 2 (after measuring the actuator):
//   1) The model now carries the measured motor gain km = 1.99.  The real
//      actuator is about twice as strong as every previous design assumed,
//      which is why R=1.0 diverged.  All gains below dropped accordingly:
//      K_theta went from -350 to -217.
//   2) Dead-zone compensation was added to the lower loop.  Below a duty of
//      about 0.25 (wheel A) / 0.35 (wheel B) the wheels do not move at all.
//
// 2026-08-16 UPDATE 3:
//   1) MsTimer2 IS GONE.  It owned Timer2, which drives the PWM on D3 and
//      D11, so analogWrite() on those two pins produced no PWM at all -
//      only the value 255 worked, because the core turns that into
//      digitalWrite(HIGH).  Measured effect: wheel B did not move for any
//      duty from 0.1 to 0.9.  Each motor could therefore only be driven in
//      ONE direction, which is what "only motor A moves" looks like from
//      the outside.  The 10 ms cycle now comes from micros(), so every
//      timer is left alone and all four motor pins really do PWM.
//   2) Q now weights the wheel angle: diag(10, 1, 1, 1, 0...) instead of
//      diag(10, 1, 0, 1, 0...).  K_wheel_angle is no longer forced to zero,
//      so the robot holds its position instead of wandering off.  The
//      closed-loop pole that used to sit exactly at 1.0 (the free wheel
//      position integrator) now sits at 0.9917.
//
//   Both changes alter the plant the gains were tuned against, so expect to
//   re-tune.  Fixing the PWM roughly doubles the usable actuation, and the
//   position feedback adds a slow mode that was not there before.
//
//   Still NOT applied: the same measurement puts tau_m at 0.44-0.82 s
//   rather than 0.070 s.  That came from wheels spinning free on a stand,
//   and folding it in would raise K_theta to about -647, so it waits for an
//   on-the-ground re-measurement.
// ============================================================

#define sign(x) ((x) < 0 ? -1 : ((x) > 0 ? 1 : 0))

